			<outline text="readme.md">
				<outline text="# BonBon">
					<outline text="&lt;i&gt;Writing is so important it deserves its own web.&lt;/i&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What is BonBon?">
					<outline text="BonBon is the first browser for the Markdown web.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It's an app that runs on MacOs and Linux. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Who">
					<outline text="I am Dave Winer, the developer of BonBon, with the help of Claude Code.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="This doc, and all other docs on this site were written by humans, unless otherwise stated.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### How to">
					<outline text="To get started, get the app from the [Download page](download/). ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Docs">
					<outline text="Docs are in the [Docs folder](docs). ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Markdown hosting">
					<outline text="GitHub is probably the best place to get free Markdown hosting. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="What we really need are a few simple desktop apps that publish Markdown files to publicly-accessible internet servers. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Open source">
					<outline text="BonBon will be released in open source as soon as things settle down.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Questions, comments">
					<outline text="We're using the [issues section](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/issues) of the GitHub repository for support. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Where we're going">
					<outline text="We're going to keep developing BonBon, and will work with developers who want to produce products that interop.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="download:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Download BonBon">
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Macintosh download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/mac.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It contains one file, BonBon.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="When you run the app, the Mac OS will require that you approve the download before you can run it.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Note: The Mac build runs only on Apple silicon.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Linux download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/linux.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It contains one folder, BonBon, which contains the program plus the files it needs beside it.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Launch it as you would any other desktop app.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Note: The Linux build runs only on Intel-type chips.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Docs">
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Background">
					<outline text="Claude and I started writing docs and didn't like anything we produced, and it was very slow going, but I decided to write the docs myself because I had delegated it to Claude for an earlier project and found the docs were so verbose and technical that I felt they were ineffective.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="So we tried a different approach with BonBon. We assumed that users had access to an AI app like Claude or ChatGPT. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="I asked Claude to write a handoff document for another instance of itself re how to help a new user find the features in BonBon that they're looking for.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The doc Claude wrote is so good that humans probably should at least skim it after trying BonBon for a bit on their own. It's really well written imho, and concise, even though when I gave it its instructions I didn't require it to be. This is how Claude talks to one of its own.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Caveat: If we add more files to this folder, unless otherwise stated, will be written by machine. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Basic AI skill doc">
					<outline text="This is the [handoff doc](bonbonSkillFromClaude.md) that I asked  Claude to write it for other AI apps, but it also will be useful to humans ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:helloworld.md">
				<outline text="# Hello world">
					<outline text="A few short steps to create a Markdown file for viewing in BonBon.">
						<outline text="* Create a new Gist on Github. Name it hello.md.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Enter some text. Use Markdown code if you like. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* In the bottom right corner of the window, choose Create public gist. [Screen shot](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/gistEditorScreenshot.png). ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Click the button. A new page displays showing a rendering of your markdown, still within GitHub. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* In the upper right corner of the window, [click Raw](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/pointingAtRaw.png). That takes us to the plain Markdown source code at a long URL, visible in the browser address bar. Copy the [URL](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/scripting/1ae7ab5a8da6accbc4202274cc4301ae/raw/18d88689d1cb43e886f5f24a2db3016aacdf997b/hello.md) to the clipboard.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Bring BonBon to the front, paste the URL into its address bar and press return. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* If all went well you'll see something [like this](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/victory.png). ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="You can put the Markdown file anywhere as long as you have a public web address for it. From there you can build structures of Markdown files, to form a web site. Not being sarcastic, just remembering that these ideas had meaning a long time ago. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</outline>
		<outline text="build script">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildBonBonRepo ()">
			</outline>
		</outline>
